In C#, the keyword `static` is used to define a member (field, property, or method) of a class that belongs to the class itself, rather than to an instance of the class.

Here are some examples:

1. Static Field: A static field is shared among all instances of a class. It is initialized only once, when the class is first loaded into memory.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static int myStaticField = 0;
}
```

2. Static Property: A static property is also shared among all instances of a class. It is accessed using the class name, not an instance of the class.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static int MyStaticProperty
    {
        get { return myStaticField; }
        set { myStaticField = value; }
    }

    private static int myStaticField = 0;
}
```

3. Static Method: A static method can only access static members (fields and other methods) of the class. It is called using the class name, not an instance of the class.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static void MyStaticMethod()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
    }
}
